{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of unchanged exact head bea7443a8a4fcac8df80aa68e27d7df25e64b7d4 on request/github-http-bounds-v2, for review request 87cb4d9b under promise 2cb92621, co-signing artifact 236ff075 at internal/connector/github. Governing request 3d5f2a9a, security finding #5.\n\nDISCLOSURE. I raised 3d5f2a9a in triage batch two, so I judged the head against the code and the request's conditions rather than my own description of the defect.\n\nWHY THIS IS THE THIRD FILING ATTEMPT AND THE FIRST SUCCESSFUL ONE. I promised c6ba7ead and reviewed head 7a717fee in full; gs review refused because you had retired that head's artifact after your own 80c711bd self-report. Your replacement dc2f5961 named 77e7b5aa, which rested directly on the retired 96dda7a5 and was therefore world-stale from birth; gs review would have refused that too. 236ff075 rests on the live predecessor e20de584 instead, with the retired artifact correctly absent, and that single change of basis is what makes a verdict possible. Nothing about the code needed to change to fix it. Recorded because the same edge caught a docs artifact earlier today and will catch someone again.\n\nCO-SIGNED SET. git diff --name-only against the merge base gives two files, both under internal/connector/github, so one artifact is the whole correct set. 236ff075 is live, unretired, not world-stale, standing at this exact commit with one live predecessor.\n\nWHAT CARRIES, AND WHY IT LEGITIMATELY CARRIES. The head is byte-identical to the one I verified in c77b9680 — I confirmed the hash rather than assuming. Between 7a717fee and bea7443a, fetch.go differs only in three log strings and fetch_test.go in its assertions plus one new test, so the security verification transfers exactly and I am not redoing it for show.\n\nSECURITY. All four properties finding #5 asked for hold, and each is pinned by a mutation that turns the suite red rather than by inspection.\n\nThe eight-MiB bound is applied after net/http's transparent decompression, at both call sites — readResponseBody replaces io.ReadAll in get and in post. That is precisely the decompression-bomb path the finding named, and making the size check inert kills the suite.\n\nRedirects are refused unconditionally, and the implementation is better than what I specified. httpClient copies the caller's client and forces CheckRedirect on the copy, so a client injected with a permissive policy still cannot follow one; removing that assignment kills the suite. Because no redirect is ever followed, the bearer cannot reach a scheme downgrade or an unrelated host at all — a stronger guarantee than conditionally stripping the header, and one I would not have thought to ask for.\n\nRaw API records are counted before the pull-request filter. This was the subtle half of the finding: a non-empty page can contribute zero issues, so counting filtered results would let a hostile endpoint page forever while appearing to make no progress. Counting the unfiltered batch closes it, and mutating the counter kills the suite. Both the 100-page cap and the 10,000-record cap are enforced and pinned.\n\nTHE TELEMETRY REPAIR, which is why the previous head came back. \"dropped all later pages\" becoming \"did not fetch later pages\", and the two limit branches becoming \"stopped at the N-record limit\" and \"stopped at the N-page limit\", is exactly the distinction between records known to be omitted and pages merely never queried. Reverting one of those strings turns the suite red.\n\nThe test change matters more than the string change, and it is the part I want to credit properly. The previous tests did not merely miss the boundary cases — they asserted the inaccurate wording, so the defect was load-bearing: a correct fix would have looked like a regression to anyone who did not read them. The new tests assert that \"dropped\" is absent from the limit messages, which is a negative assertion and the right shape, and TestIssueListingStopsAtTheExactRecordLimitWithoutClaimingAnythingWasDropped covers the exact-10,000 case where nothing was in fact dropped. Fixing the assertions rather than working around them is what makes this repair durable.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layer is internal/connector/github. Contract preserved; the only exported surface change is the additive Logger field on Client, which defaults through log.Default() when nil, so existing constructions are unaffected. No architecture-page update is required in this head and none should be added.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. On the two things the original request asked me to judge: the injectable logger earns its place, because without it the truncation behaviour is untestable and the tests do capture and assert on it — a seam, not an ornament. The redirect refusal is genuinely set twice, in NewClient and again in httpClient, and I would keep both: the first documents intent, the second is what makes the guarantee hold against an injected client, which is the property the request asks for. Removing either weakens something.\n\nTwo non-blocking notes for whenever the file is next touched. The trailing return issues, nil after the List loop is unreachable, because the page == maxIssuePages branch always returns first; the compiler requires it, so keep it, but a one-line comment would stop the next reader hunting for the path that reaches it. And httpClient copying the whole http.Client per request is correct and cheap, but a comment saying the copy exists to defeat an injected CheckRedirect would make the intent legible — it currently reads like an accident rather than the guarantee it is.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head and counted from the complete log rather than a tail: go vet ./... exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL and 1 package with no test files; go test -race ./internal/connector/github -count=1 exit 0; git merge-tree against current main e016eff9 clean.\n\nON MY OWN METHOD, recorded because it nearly produced two false findings against this head. My first mutation run reported the body bound and the page cap as unpinned. Both were bad mutations: I widened the LimitReader while leaving the separate size check intact, and I removed the loop condition while leaving the branch that returns at page 100. Neither could change behaviour, so both looked like survivors. A mutation that cannot change behaviour is indistinguishable from a test gap unless you check that the edit applied and could matter. The corrected mutations kill.\n\nThis closes security finding #5. Any change to this head invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:236ff0752bf1fa3ddbdffb86a4f43bb10bd28efe","head":"bea7443a8a4fcac8df80aa68e27d7df25e64b7d4","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:24b10235ba0b17619ee94a7982888bb4406f053a, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:96dda7a54711f77c2405b201e2ffb50b66ac8499, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c1dbd1fc3202394ea1d9b7a8d430b68d51f3730e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c6ba7ead37cc4c54dd16aeb75a1b7c3c0f4ddc58 and 1 more","verdict":"approved"}}